      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Stress radio nuclide ventriculography is used to determine ejection fraction and changes in regional wall motion for the detection of ischemia-induced left ventricular dysfunction in patients with coronary artery disease. We have evaluated the relative sensitivity of regional wall motion and of ejection fraction during hand-grip and cold pressor test, in 21 patients with angina pectoris and documented coronary artery disease at angiography. Regional wall motion appeared a more sensitive index than global ejection fraction producing only two false negative cases. Ejection fraction showed great variability among the patients during both hand-grip and cold pressor test. This might be explained by the fact that both tests do not produce a sufficient degree of ischemic stress on the left ventricle. Hand-grip and cold pressor tests appear useful alternative to dynamic exercise in the diagnosis of coronary artery disease being also safe and simple to perform."},
